    {
      "prompt_id": "c3737bdaf751152684d697ef",
      "query": "Question:\nIn the FileManager class, which of the following wrongly describes the purpose of the write_with_template method, and how it handles file writing while ensuring template substitution?\n\nChoices:\nA. Template Substitution Handling: The write_with_template method calls substitute_with_template to replace patterns in the template file using only the dictionary. The result is then written to the file only if dry_run is False.\nB. Duplicate File Write Prevention: The write_with_template method maintains a set called filenames that tracks written files. It raises an assertion error if the same file is attempted to be written again within the same execution.\nC. File Writing if Contents Changed: The _write_if_changed method checks if the contents of a file have changed by comparing them with the new contents. If they differ, it overwrites the file, ensuring only modified files are rewritten.\nD. Generated Comment Insertion: When the env_callable returns a dictionary, the substitute_with_template method checks if the key generated_comment exists. If it does not, it creates one using the generator path and the template filename. This is added to the dictionary before substitution.\n\nEnd with exactly one line: Answer: X",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "label": "Callable environment can return dict OR string, disproving only dictionary",
          "token_span": [
            55913,
            56126
          ],
          "L20_C4_rows": 54,
          "missing_base": 22,
          "missing_final": 0
        },
        {
          "label": "Duplicate prevention, dry-run and writing call",
          "token_span": [
            56125,
            56256
          ],
          "L20_C4_rows": 33,
          "missing_base": 15,
          "missing_final": 0
        }
      ],
      "correct_choice": "A",
      "outputs": {
        "full": {
          "choice": "A",
          "completion": "The `write_with_template` method in the `FileManager` class calls `substitute_with_template`, which can handle both a dictionary and a string. If it's a dictionary, it adds a `generated_comment` if missing. The method then writes the file only if `dry_run` is `False`. It also tracks filenames to prevent duplicates. The `_write_if_changed` method compares old and new contents and only writes if they differ.  \n\nOption A says \"using only the dictionary,\" but `substitute_with_template` can also handle a string return from `env_callable`. This makes A incorrect in its description.  \n\nAnswer: A"
        },
        "task_aware_60": {
          "choice": "A",
          "completion": "The `write_with_template` method in the `FileManager` class calls `substitute_with_template`, which can handle both a dictionary and a string. If it's a dictionary, it adds a `generated_comment` if missing, then substitutes. The result is written only if `dry_run` is `False`. It also tracks filenames to prevent duplicates. The `_write_if_changed` method compares old and new contents and overwrites only if different.  \n\nOption A says \"using only the dictionary\" which is incorrect because `substitute_with_template` can also handle a string return from `env_callable`.  \n\nAnswer: A"
        },
        "query_free_60": {
          "choice": "C",
          "completion": "The `write_with_template` method in the `FileManager` class does not call `_write_if_changed`; it directly writes the file if `dry_run` is `False`. The `_write_if_changed` method is not defined in the provided code. Therefore, option C is incorrect.  \n\nAnswer: C"
        },
        "query_free_recovery": {
          "choice": "A",
          "completion": "Answer: A"
        }
      },
